      "id": 1,
      "text": "South Heart, a small North Dakota town, was founded in 1908 during the early 20th‑century expansion of the Northern Pacific Railway.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/South_Heart%2C_North_Dakota"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s name comes from its position south of the Heart River.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://schs.omeka.net/items/show/4245"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The first permanent settlement began in 1881 with a Northern Pacific station built 1.5 miles west of where the town now sits.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://schs.omeka.net/items/show/4245"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In 1910 the Northern Pacific station and school‑house were relocated to the current site, and a section house accommodated two families.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://schs.omeka.net/items/show/4245"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The first white child born in Stark County, Thomas, was the son of the town’s first settler.",
